<description>
Remove each fracture marker line and push the surrounding object pixels one cell away from it.

Input structure
- A background grid with 8-connected foreground objects made from a body color and a rarer marker color.
- Each object has a non-square bbox; the marker pixels form one complete fracture line across the shorter bbox axis.

Rule
- Erase all marker pixels.
- For a wide object, each row has one marker; body pixels left of it shift one cell left and body pixels right of it shift one cell right.
- For a tall object, each column has one marker; body pixels above it shift one cell up and body pixels below it shift one cell down.

Key insight
- The marker line is a crack through the object, so the two sides separate by one pixel perpendicular to the crack.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="f8f52ecc" lines="107-122">
<description>
Connect same-colored foreground components with one-cell-wide orthogonal paths around teal wall segments.

Input structure
- The dominant color is the background.
- Teal cells are fixed wall segments and are never source connectors.
- Other foreground colors appear as one or more 4-connected components; colors with a single component stay unchanged.

Rule
- For each non-background, non-teal color, read its 4-connected components in row-major order.
- Connect each consecutive pair by the shortest clear L-shaped path between the two components.
- Clear paths may pass only through background or the same color, and they preserve the previous travel axis when both bends are possible.

Key insight
- The output is not a global recolor: each color traces a routed chain through its own components while teal cells and other colors act as blockers.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="22425bda" lines="58-76">
<description>
List the colors of overlaid straight lines in their only possible bottom-to-top crossing order.

Input structure
The background is the dominant color. Every foreground color is the visible part
of one full one-cell-thick horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree diagonal line.
Higher lines may hide pixels of lower lines at crossings.

Rule
Recover each full line from its visible collinear pixels. At any cell where
multiple recovered lines cross, the visible color is above every other line
through that cell. Return the unique ordering of all line colors that respects
those above/below constraints and whose consecutive colors are lines that
geometrically cross.

Key insight
The visible crossing colors give a partial layer order, and the intended answer
is the unique Hamiltonian chain through the line-intersection graph from the
bottom layer to the top layer.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="df978a02" lines="57-72">
<description>
Let the largest center-pointing object grow outward while every other object loses its inward tip.

Input structure
- The grid has a single dominant background color and four 8-connected monochromatic foreground objects.
- The objects surround a central background gap and point toward it from cardinal directions.
- Exactly one object has the largest area.

Rule
- Find the central background gap by minimizing aggregate distance to the four objects.
- Recolor the nearest-to-center pixel of each non-largest object to background.
- Add a three-pixel cap in the largest object's color on the side away from the center, centered on the shared row or column.

Key insight
- The edit is a transfer of prominence around a common center: losers retract at their tips, and the unique largest object extends outward from the center.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="5b37cb25" lines="59-73">
<description>
Fill each plus-shaped background notch with the color of the frame side it opens toward.

Input structure
- A one-cell-thick rectangular frame has four solid-colored sides and neutral corner pixels.
- The frame interior contains a dominant background color and one foreground object color.
- Some object boundaries contain five-pixel plus-shaped background notches.

Rule
- Find every background plus whose opposite side is supported by foreground object pixels and whose open side faces one frame side.
- Recolor the five pixels of that plus with the color of the facing frame side.
- Leave all other pixels unchanged.

Key insight
The frame side colors are direction labels for the open notches in the foreground object.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="e048c9ed" lines="11-26">
<description>
Add a quantity-color label in the top marker column for each horizontal bar.

Input structure
- The grid has a black background and one non-background marker pixel in the top row.
- Below the marker row, every non-empty source row contains one solid monochrome horizontal bar to the left of the marker column.
- Source rows are staggered every other row.

Rule
- Copy the input grid.
- For each source bar, read its quantity as the bar length.
- Paint the quantity color in the marker column on the same row as the bar:
  2->1, 3->4, 4->9, 5->6.

Key insight
- The grey marker supplies the result column, while each bar supplies a small quantity with a fixed color code.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="a1aa0c1e" lines="74-97">
<description>
Compress the stacked colored bars into a compact ordered table with the control pixel on the selected row.

Input structure
- A background grid contains several full-width colored bars from top to
  bottom; the last bar is the base/separator color.
- Each non-base bar may have an aligned three-column comb motif below it,
  alternating side-pair rows with solid rows.
- A singleton control pixel sits away from the colored motifs.

Rule
- Make one output row per non-base bar, preserving top-to-bottom order.
- In that row, paint the bar color once for each solid three-pixel row in
  its motif, then paint the base-bar color in the separator column.
- The singleton control pixel sits immediately above the base bar. Among
  motifs whose open side-pair parity is compatible with that bottom row,
  put the control pixel in the output row with the fewest solid rows; use
  horizontal distance to the motif center only to break count ties.

Key insight
- The vertical comb height is a count, while the control pixel is read by
  projecting it onto the center gap of compatible comb rows rather than by
  its vertical band alone.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="18447a8d" lines="33-48">
<description>
Move each right-side colored shape into the left slot whose template color is its exact complement.

Input structure:
- The grid has two equal-width slots separated by one background column.
- Non-background rows form horizontal bands separated by all-background rows.
- Each band has a shared template color in the left slot and one colored source shape in the right slot.

Rule:
- For each band, define its destination shape as the cells in the left slot not occupied by the template color.
- Normalize every right-side source shape to right-slot coordinates and match it to the unique equal destination shape.
- Clear the original right-side source pixels and paint the matched left-slot complement with the source color.

Key insight:
The right-side objects are not placed by row order; each one completes the unique left template whose missing cells have the same shape.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="b25e450b" lines="28-43">
<description>
Slide each black border rectangle to the opposite border, recoloring its swept stripe orange and leaving the rectangle black at the far edge.

Input structure
- The grid contains black rectangular 4-connected masks, each touching exactly one outer border.
- Grey cells form incidental pattern content; orange is the trail color used when a black mask slides.

Rule
- Treat all black rectangles as moving simultaneously.
- A rectangle touching the top or bottom edge moves vertically to the opposite edge in the same columns.
- A rectangle touching the left or right edge moves horizontally to the opposite edge in the same rows.
- Recolor the complete swept row or column stripe to orange, then stamp every destination rectangle in black.

Key insight
- Edge contact gives the slide direction; the output is the set of opposite-edge black rectangles plus their orange trails.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="9b30e358" lines="9-23">
<description>
Repeat the bottom foreground motif upward until it fills the whole grid.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- The only foreground pixels occupy a contiguous block of rows at the bottom.
- That bottom block is the complete vertical motif, including its background cells.

Rule
- Copy the bottom motif rows repeatedly upward across the full grid.
- Preserve horizontal alignment and vertical phase: the original first motif row remains at its input row.
- The output shape is unchanged.

Key insight
The blank rows above the bottom foreground block are missing repetitions of the same row motif, not empty space.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

